[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined"]
[Variation "Orthodox Defence, Capablanca's freeing manoeuvre"]
[ECO "D63"]
[Character "solid,positional"]
[Risk "low"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "pin"]
[Themes "freeing-trades,pin-on-f6"]
[For "black"]
[Kind "main"]
[Opponent "3.Nc3"]

1. d4 d5 { Black takes a share of the centre at once. The set-up in this course (...Nf6, ...e6, ...c5, ...Nc6) works against nearly every White move that is not 2.c4. } 2. c4 { Offers the c-pawn to pull Black's d-pawn away from the centre. Black cannot hold the extra pawn for long, so this is a safe way to fight for e4 and d5. } 2... e6 { Black guards the d5 pawn with a pawn and opens the bishop on f8, instead of taking the gambit pawn. } 3. Nc3 { Adds a second attacker on d5, so Black must keep defending it. } 3... Nf6 4. Bg5 { Pins the knight on f6 to the queen. If it moves, White takes on d5 with more force. } 4... Nbd7 { A second defender for the pinned knight, and it clears the c-pawn for ...c6. } 5. e3 Be7 { Puts the bishop between the g5 bishop and the queen, which ends the pin and gets ready to castle. } 6. Nf3 O-O { Castle now: the king is safe and the rook looks at the centre. } 7. Rc1 ( 7. Qc2 c6 8. cxd5 exd5 { Recapture with the e-pawn so the c-file and the bishop stay open. } ) ( 7. Bd3 dxc4 8. Bxc4 c5 ) 7... c6 { Guards d5 and b5, so Black can take on c4 later without giving up the centre. } 8. Bd3 ( 8. cxd5 exd5 ) 8... dxc4 { Black waited until the bishop had used a move on d3. Now Bxc4 costs White a second bishop move, so Black gains time. } 9. Bxc4 Nd5 { Black has less space, so offering trades helps: each swap gives the remaining pieces more room. } 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 11. O-O Nxc3 12. Rxc3 e5 { The freeing break: with the centre open, Black's pieces have room. Next bring the c8 bishop out, keep the e5 pawn guarded and trade a pair of rooks. } *
